      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 3, 4, 6, and 7. The LCM of a set of numbers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the numbers in the set.\n\nThe LCM of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) can be found using their greatest common divisor (GCD) with the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = \\frac{|a \\cdot b|}{\\text{GCD}(a, b)}\n\\]\n\nWe will use this formula for three numbers and once more for the four numbers to ensure accuracy. Let's start with the three numbers (3, 4, 6):\n\n1. Compute the GCD of 3, 4, and 6.\n2. Compute the LCM using the formula above.\n\nNext, we will do the same for the four numbers (3, 4, 6, 7):\n\n1. Compute the GCD of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n2. Compute the LCM using the formula above.\n\nLet's write the Python code to perform these calculations:\n```python\nimport math\nfrom functools import reduce\n\n# Function to compute GCD of a list of numbers\ndef gcd_multiple(numbers):\n    return reduce(math.gcd, numbers)\n\n# Function to compute LCM of a list of numbers\ndef lcm(a, b):\n    return abs(a * b) // math.gcd(a, b)\n\ndef lcm_multiple(numbers):\n    return reduce(lcm, numbers)\n\n# Numbers for the LCM calculation\nnumbers_3_4_6 = [3, 4, 6]\nnumbers_3_4_6_7 = [3, 4, 6, 7]\n\n# Compute the LCM for the three numbers\nlcm_3_4_6 = lcm_multiple(numbers_3_4_6)\n\n# Compute the LCM for the four numbers\nlcm_3_4_6_7 = lcm_multiple(numbers_3_4_6_7)\n\nprint((lcm_3_4_6, lcm_3_4_6_7))\n```\n```output\n(12, 84)\n```\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 3, 4, and 6 is 12. This means they will next all be working together in 12 days. However, we need to find the LCM of the numbers 3, 4, 6, and 7, which is the LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7 because Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi work every third, fourth, sixth, and seventh school day respectively.\n\nThe LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7 is 84. Therefore, they will next all be working together in \\(\\boxed{84}\\) school days.",
